NAME
       hashcrack - try a word list against password hashes (Cyber Range)

SYNOPSIS
       hashcrack [--format=FORMAT] [--wordlist=FILE] HASHFILE
       hashcrack --show HASHFILE

DESCRIPTION
       A password hash can't be turned back into the password, but a guess can
       be hashed the same way and compared. hashcrack does that for every word
       of a word list (default /usr/share/wordlists/range-common.txt, a few
       hundred weak passwords). It finds only passwords that are in the list:
       that is why long, unusual passwords are safe from it.

       Each line of HASHFILE is a hash, USER:HASH (as in /etc/shadow), or
       USER:RID:LM:NT::: (a Windows-style export). hashcrack recognises:

           $6$...         the simulator's /etc/shadow hashes (sha512crypt-sim)
           $rangetgs$...  service tickets from ticket-request(1)
           32 hex digits  md5 (or a simulated Windows hash with --format=nt;
                          the USER:RID:LM:NT::: lines are read as nt)
           40 hex digits  sha1
           64 hex digits  sha256

       The shadow, Windows and ticket formats are Cyber Range's own pretend
       formats: they only mean something inside the range.

       Passwords it found are kept in ~/.hashcrack.pot, so --show prints them
       again without trying the list.

OPTIONS
       --wordlist=FILE, -w FILE   the words to try
       --format=FORMAT, -f FORMAT md5, sha1, sha256, nt, sha512crypt or rangetgs
       --show                     print USER:PASSWORD for the hashes found

EXAMPLES
       hashcrack shadow-backup.txt
       hashcrack --format=nt sam-export.txt
       hashcrack --show shadow-backup.txt

SEE ALSO
       pwguess(1), sha256sum(1), ticket-request(1)
